  "limitations": [
   "Horizons are ray-marched up to 120 km along great circles of the 1737.4 km reference sphere on the LOLA 80 m/px DEM; features smaller than the grid (boulders, sub-80 m relief) need LROC NAC imagery before any touchdown assessment.",
   "Illumination, energy, battery and the power score cover only 2026-11-15T00:00:00Z -> 2026-11-16T00:00:00Z (1.0 days, step 3600 s); the sub-solar latitude in MOON_ME ranged -1.500 to -1.500 deg (full seasonal excursion about +/-1.59 deg in DE421). The window is shorter than one seasonal cycle (~346.62 d, eclipse year): these are season-specific values, not annual statistics. The whole window lies on the southern-summer side (sub-solar latitude < 0), the most favourable season for south-polar illumination.",
   "Relay trajectories propagate two-body Keplerian motion anchored to the lunar equatorial frame at the window start; Earth third-body and lunar gravity-field perturbations are excluded over the 1.0-day window.",
   "No atmospheric refraction exists on the Moon; solar panel degradation from dust/temperature is excluded at an unsourced baseline efficiency eta=0.28 and an unsourced 100 W survival load.",
   "Score fields are an ILLUSTRATIVE landing index: weights, blends and thresholds have no cited source and no sensitivity analysis. They are not a validated scientific ranking.",
   "slope_deg is the gradient over a 400 m baseline (central differences of +/-1 pixel); it does not resolve lander-scale slopes. Points with slope above clps_slope_limit_deg = 12 deg (an unsourced placeholder) are eliminated: landing score 0.",
   "is_psr and psr_distance_m share one criterion: horizon >= solar-center envelope (sub-solar latitude -1.591 deg) + 0.272 deg solar radius at every azimuth, using horizons computed at 2 m observer height (not ground level). PSR points are searched on a symmetric 1000 m lattice within 5200 m of each site center (flagged grid nodes included); psr_distance_m is null when no PSR point was found within that radius (unknown distance; it gets no proximity credit). Node distances only consider that lattice, so a closer PSR outside it can be missed.",
   "Relay presets are hypothetical geometry scenarios. 'lro' reuses LRO's 2009-2011 50 km circular polar mapping-orbit geometry (Slojkowski, 24th ISSFD 2014) with an arbitrary initial phase; LRO is not a relay service and its 2026 orbit differs. The ELFO elements (a=5740 km, e=0.58, i=57.8 deg, omega=90 deg), phases, 3-satellite phasing, 5 deg mask and ranges are unsourced; only the orbit class (ELFO) and the 2200-2290 MHz S-band return link of ESA/SSTL Lunar Pathfinder are published (SpaceOps 2023, paper 339). LunaNet is a framework, not this constellation.",
   "Communications are line-of-sight only (terrain horizon, 0 deg DTE mask vs 5 deg relay mask, range limit, lunar occultation); no link budget, ground-station visibility or interference. Visibility is sampled hourly: outages shorter than one hour are not resolved, and the hourly lro_visible series aliases the ~1.9 h LRO-like period (lro_duty_cycle_pct at site centers uses a 5-minute grid).",
   "With the hypothetical ELFO constellation enabled, combined_dte_elfo_duty_cycle_pct can saturate at 100 % for every site, which floors the illustrative comms score at 65/100 regardless of the site; compare with DTE-only values.",
   "'Validated' for this dataset means conformance with navigator-dataset.v1.schema.json. The only external comparison is provenance.horizons_validation (Sun/Earth directions at one site)."
  ]
